/*******************************************************************\
|                     INSTALLATION INSTRUCTIONS                     |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+


+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
| QUICK START                                                       |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+

o REQUIREMENTS

  The simulator requires these libraries:

    - SDL2
    - SDL2_TTF

o COMPILATION

  The make in the top level directory recognizes these targets:

    - all builds everything (default)
    - bin builds the binary executable in bin
    - install-data copies ROMs, font, logos, games.lst and tapes into
      the flat directory PMD_SHARE

  Example (PMD_SHARE is compiled in, trailing slash required):

    make PMD_BUILD=RELEASE PMD_SHARE=$HOME/share/
    make install-data PMD_SHARE=$HOME/share/
    PMD_WEB=8085 bin/simpmd

  Saved states (Alt+F5 / Alt+F9) go to <PMD_SHARE>states by default. To keep them
  in the home directory of every user, give a directory relative to $HOME:

    make PMD_BUILD=RELEASE PMD_SHARE=/usr/share/simpmd/ PMD_STATEHOME=.local/share/simpmd

  (run make clean after changing it; PMD_STATEDIR of the environment overrides both)

  Standard (debug) build, run from bin (the data are read from the
  current directory):

    make
    make install-data PMD_SHARE=bin/
    cd bin && ./simpmd

o EXECUTION

  The simulator requires that the ROMs M1 and M2 (here M2-Patched-Tape),
  the font, logos, games.lst and the tapes are present in PMD_SHARE
  (release build) or the current directory (standard build).


+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
| C VERSION                                                         |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+

The C version of the simulator has been tested on Fedora Core Linux.
It should be reasonably portable on most Linux platforms, feel free
to contact me if any issues come up.

